WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.838
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:09.803
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.103 --> 00:00:15.716
Unit three: Entrepreneurship. Today you will describe qualities with so ... that and such ... that.

4
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.738
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.863
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:24.590
Startup.

7
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:28.605
Startup means a new business built on a new idea.

8
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:31.425
The startup began in a small garage.

9
00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:34.402
Your turn. Say: Startup.

10
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:38.256
Startup.

11
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:40.111
Venture capital.

12
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:45.067
Venture capital means money given to young companies with high growth potential.

13
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:48.530
Venture capital helped the firm hire engineers.

14
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.222
Your turn. Say: Venture capital.

15
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:56.370
Venture capital.

16
00:00:57.117 --> 00:00:58.489
Business plan.

17
00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:03.480
Business plan means a document describing how a business will make money.

18
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:06.396
The bank asked to see her business plan.

19
00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:09.961
Your turn. Say: Business plan.

20
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.929
Business plan.

21
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:15.754
Risk-taking.

22
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.934
Risk-taking means being willing to try something that may fail.

23
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.828
Risk-taking is part of every startup.

24
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:26.011
Your turn. Say: Risk-taking.

25
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:30.193
Risk-taking.

26
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.114
Innovation.

27
00:01:33.117 --> 00:01:35.882
Innovation means a new idea, method or product.

28
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:39.098
Innovation separates leaders from followers.

29
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:42.069
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

30
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:46.004
Innovation.

31
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:47.914
Market gap.

32
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:52.426
Market gap means a need that no company yet satisfies.

33
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:55.539
She found a market gap in rural delivery.

34
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.139
Your turn. Say: Market gap.

35
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.268
Market gap.

36
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:05.076
Funding round.

37
00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:10.080
Funding round means a stage when a company raises money from investors.

38
00:02:10.683 --> 00:02:13.205
The second funding round raised five million.

39
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:16.353
Your turn. Say: Funding round.

40
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.352
Funding round.

41
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:21.990
Scale.

42
00:02:22.983 --> 00:02:26.214
Scale means to grow a business without losing quality.

43
00:02:26.817 --> 00:02:29.455
They plan to scale across Southeast Asia.

44
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:32.702
Your turn. Say: Scale.

45
00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:36.649
Scale.

46
00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:41.456
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:48.475
Visal, a news feature says an entrepreneur was 'so determined that she sold her car'. What is that structure?

48
00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:59.030
So + adjective + that shows a result. With nouns we use such: 'It was such a strong idea that investors queued up.'

49
00:03:00.117 --> 00:03:02.488
Which qualities does the feature praise?

50
00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:12.180
Risk-taking, innovation and a clear business plan. She spotted a market gap that was so obvious that nobody saw it.

51
00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:15.631
And what happens after the funding round?

52
00:03:16.717 --> 00:03:23.107
The startup tries to scale. Success can be so rapid that managing growth becomes the next challenge.

53
00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.988
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:31.044
Grammar focus: Result clauses: so  that and such  that.

55
00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:32.566
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.387
She was so determined that she sold her car to start the firm.

57
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:40.339
It was such a strong idea that investors queued up.

58
00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.341
The growth was so rapid that managing it became difficult.

59
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:52.594
Use so + adjective/adverb and such + (a/an) + adjective + noun before 'that': so rapid that..., such a good plan that...

60
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:57.036
Be careful! Don't say: It was so good idea that we won.

61
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.696
Say: It was such a good idea that we won.

62
00:04:01.996 --> 00:04:04.983
Use such + a/an + adjective + noun.

63
00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:08.233
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:10.476
Making a Startup Work.

65
00:04:10.776 --> 00:04:14.250
Entrepreneurs notice a market gap that other people overlook.

66
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:19.648
Their innovation may be so simple that rivals wonder why they did not think of it first.

67
00:04:20.633 --> 00:04:25.489
A clear business plan and some risk-taking attract venture capital in a funding round.

68
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.671
Success can be so fast that the company must scale before its systems are ready.

69
00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:36.545
Resilience is therefore such an important quality that investors often judge founders by it.

70
00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:41.600
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.053
She was blank determined that she sold her car.

72
00:04:45.353 --> 00:04:48.449
A, so. B, such. C, very that.

73
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.721
The answer is A: so.

74
00:04:54.883 --> 00:04:57.661
It was blank a strong idea that investors queued up.

75
00:04:57.961 --> 00:05:01.428
A, so. B, such. C, too.

76
00:05:04.967 --> 00:05:07.094
The answer is B: such.

77
00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.167
A need that no company satisfies is a market blank.

78
00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:14.447
A, gap. B, round. C, plan.

79
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.756
The answer is A: gap.

80
00:05:20.917 --> 00:05:22.742
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:23.042 --> 00:05:29.749
A, It was so good idea that we won.. B, It was such a good idea that we won.. C, It was such good idea that we won..

82
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.437
The answer is B: It was such a good idea that we won..

83
00:05:37.583 --> 00:05:41.324
According to the reading, investors often judge founders by…

84
00:05:41.624 --> 00:05:44.883
A, resilience. B, age. C, luck.

85
00:05:48.433 --> 00:05:51.037
The answer is A: resilience.

86
00:05:52.233 --> 00:05:55.640
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:56.483 --> 00:05:59.632
What qualities do you think a successful entrepreneur needs?

88
00:06:08.417 --> 00:06:09.836
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:36.431
A successful entrepreneur needs creativity to spot a market gap and courage for risk-taking. However, determination matters just as much, because most startups face failure before success. Good entrepreneurs are also so organised that they can follow a clear business plan, and they are open to advice. Above all, they learn from their mistakes.

90
00:06:36.731 --> 00:06:38.313
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:39.217 --> 00:06:44.586
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and result clauses: so  that and such  that.

92
00:06:44.886 --> 00:06:49.152
Today's value is resourcefulness. Make more from less and waste nothing.

93
00:06:49.452 --> 00:06:51.942
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:06:52.242 --> 00:06:55.219
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

